<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Around 10 years ago, Agol and Krushkal showed that the number of chromatic polynomials <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> arising from graphs <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices grows exponentially with <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, by establishing that the (dual) flow polynomial <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{G}\left(\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{G}\left(\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> already takes on exponentially many values, if one varies <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over all planar cubic graphs <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We show, more generally, that the size of the set <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\}</annotation></semantics></math> is exponential in <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, for every fixed real number <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\neq 0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\neq 0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, our approach can also be pushed to show that <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> already takes on exponentially many values, if we only vary <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> over all planar graphs on <math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. The case <math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math> confirms a conjecture of Agol, which was initially motivated by the <span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">NP</span>-completeness of planar <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-colorability.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>–coloring</em> of a finite undirected graph <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is an assignment
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:V\to[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:V\to[q]</annotation></semantics></math> such that adjacent vertices receive different colors.
When <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of proper <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>–colorings is denoted <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is a classical theorem of Birkhoff and Whitney that there is a unique polynomial <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x)\in\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> which encodes the number of proper <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-colorings of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> when evaluated at <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=q</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the so-called <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chromatic polynomial</span> of graph <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a polynomial of degree <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> and with leading coefficient equal to <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. This object was first introduced by Birkhoff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> in 1912 as part of an algebraic approach for the Four Color Problem. Birkhoff’s original motivation was to study colorings of planar maps by encoding them in a polynomial and analyzing its behavior at small integer values, particularly at <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Although this approach did not yield a proof of the Four Color Theorem, it initiated a rich line of research and established the chromatic polynomial as a fundamental graph invariant. Whitney <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> subsequently extended Birkhoff’s ideas to general graphs and introduced many of the structural properties of chromatic polynomials that are now standard. Among these is the deletion–contraction identity: for any edge <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-e</annotation></semantics></math> denotes deletion and <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/e</annotation></semantics></math> denotes contraction.
This identity places chromatic polynomials within the broader framework of the Tutte polynomial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The chromatic polynomial encodes substantial information about <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
For instance, the chromatic number satisfies</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)=\min\{q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}:P_{G}(q)&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)=\min\{q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}:P_{G}(q)&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Evaluations at negative integers also have a rich combinatorial meaning:
Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> showed that</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{G}(-1)|=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>acyclic orientations of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{G}(-1)|=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and more generally his chromatic reciprocity theorem interprets <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{|V|}P_{G}(-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{|V|}P_{G}(-m)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
as a weighted count of acyclic orientations together with order-preserving maps to <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chromatic polynomial spectrum.</span> In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Agol and Krushkal proved that the number of chromatic polynomials of planar triangulations on <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices grows exponentially with <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In a few words, their argument works with duals of planar cubic graphs and considers the number of distinct values taken by the <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">flow polynomial</em> at the special value
<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(3-\sqrt{5})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(3-\sqrt{5})/2</annotation></semantics></math> (for a planar graph <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, its flow polynomial is precisely <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G^{*}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G^{*}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>). At this particular parameter <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the chromatic algebra (a certain algebra over <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}[Q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}[Q]</annotation></semantics></math> formed from linear combinations of isotopy classes of planar graphs in a rectangle with a fixed number of univalent points on top and bottom) satisfies an additional local relation (coming from Temperley–Lieb / Jones–Wenzl theory),
which collapses a natural boundary module from dimension three to dimension two.
Two explicit local operations on planar graphs <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> then act on <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G^{*}}(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G^{*}}(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> matrices whose squares generate a free semigroup,
yielding exponentially many distinct evaluations and hence exponentially many distinct chromatic polynomials.
See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> for more details and further references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we study the asymptotics of the <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">evaluation spectra</em> of the chromatic polynomial. First, let <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mG_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mG_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all simple graphs on <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. For each fixed real number <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we can now define</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}\,\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}\,\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In words, <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of distinct values attained by <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over all simple graphs on <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Furthermore, let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\,\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\;\coloneqq\;\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\,\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mG_{n}^{\pl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mG_{n}^{\pl}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex, simple, planar graphs. Trivially, <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(q)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(q)\right|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our results.</span>
We establish lower bounds for <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> for arbitrary fixed <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. For integer <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the value <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> counts proper <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>–colorings, and Agol conjectured that the spectrum <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(3)</annotation></semantics></math> should also grows exponentially over <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mG_{n}^{\pl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mG_{n}^{\pl}</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> for more context, in particular a motivation coming from the <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">NP</span>-completeness of <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>–colorability of planar graphs.
Our main theorem confirms this conjecture and, more generally, shows that the evaluation spectrum is exponential in <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for every fixed
<math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every real number <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the evaluation spectrum <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, we can show the following uniform lower bounds:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\begin{cases}2^{(n-2)/2},&amp;q&lt;0,\\[4.0pt]
2^{n/8-o(n)},&amp;\hskip-11.38109pt0&lt;q&lt;2,\\[4.0pt]
\sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{(n-2)/2}),&amp;q&gt;2,\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\begin{cases}2^{(n-2)/2},&amp;q&lt;0,\\[4.0pt]
2^{n/8-o(n)},&amp;\hskip-11.38109pt0&lt;q&lt;2,\\[4.0pt]
\sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{(n-2)/2}),&amp;q&gt;2,\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F_{n})_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{n})_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fibonacci sequence and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2</annotation></semantics></math> is the golden ratio.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and the trivial inequality <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(-1)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)\right|\geq\left|\smash{\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}}(-1)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> together imply that <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq 2^{(n-2)/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{n}|\geq 2^{(n-2)/2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This fact also improves quantitatively upon the lower bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, the lower bounds from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> hold true even if we additionally require the family of (planar) graphs in <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mG_{n}^{\pl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mG_{n}^{\pl}</annotation></semantics></math> to be connected. See Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Remark 2.11. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Before we say more about the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, let us first remark that for <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> the spectrum is not exponential even if we allow <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> to vary over <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> graphs on <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.
Indeed,</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for every graph with at least one vertex, so <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(0)|=|\mathsf{S}_{n}(0)|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(0)|=|\mathsf{S}_{n}(0)|=1</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is edgeless and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, so <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(1)|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(1)|=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(1)|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(1)|=2</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(2)</annotation></semantics></math> counts proper <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>–colorings, so it is <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for non-bipartite <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and equals <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{c(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{c(G)}</annotation></semantics></math> for bipartite <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(G)</annotation></semantics></math> connected components.
Hence <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(2)|\leq|\mathsf{S}_{n}(2)|=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(2)|\leq|\mathsf{S}_{n}(2)|=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is more delicate when <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math> (and precise quantitative bounds are also more difficult to extract), so, as a prequel to that side of the story, we will first use an elementary argument to show that <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> is exponential in that range, by reducing the problem to the case when <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We record this as a second theorem, in greater generality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every non-integer real number <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-\lceil q\rceil-2)/2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-\lceil q\rceil-2)/2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, then Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> immediately implies that <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\geq 2^{(n-4)/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\geq 2^{(n-4)/2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Last but not least, let us also emphasize that for positive integers <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, exponential growth for <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\right|</annotation></semantics></math> is best possible, up to the correct base of the exponent. Indeed, if <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> then every <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex graph has at most <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> proper <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>–colorings, so <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a nonnegative integer, there is however no comparable a priori bound of the form <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{G}(q)|\leq C^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{G}(q)|\leq C^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, Stanley’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> gives <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n!</annotation></semantics></math>, and, more generally, it is not difficult to show that for any fixed <math id="S1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> the complete graph satisfies
<math id="S1.p11.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{K_{n}}(q)=(q)_{n}=q(q-1)\cdots(q-n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{K_{n}}(q)=(q)_{n}=q(q-1)\cdots(q-n+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose magnitude grows on the order of <math id="S1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n!</annotation></semantics></math>.
These factorial-scale values suggest that, at least for some non-integer or negative points, the spectrum itself should be <em id="S1.p11.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">superexponential</em>. We will discuss this some more in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Concluding remarks ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related work and proof ideas.</span>
A closely related spectrum problem was recently resolved for the spanning-tree enumerator.
Let <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of spanning trees of <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and set</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(n)\coloneqq\{\tau(G):G\in\mG_{n}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(n)\coloneqq\{\tau(G):G\in\mG_{n}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">as well as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)\coloneqq\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>pl</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)\coloneqq\{\,P_{G}(q)\,:\,G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, Chan, Kontorovich and Pak proved that <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>pl</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)|</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially in <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, via a connection to continued fractions, thin orbits, and a deep result of Bourgain-Kontorovich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> on the so-called Zaremba conjecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and the references therein for more information. Soon after, Alon–Bucić–Gishboliner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> gave a much simpler alternative proof, which also came with improved quantitative bounds and results for other classes of graphs. Both arguments made crucial use of the deletion–contraction formula for the spanning-tree enumerator <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and a handful of basic operations that increase the number of vertices of the graph, and which allow one to track the evolution of the parameters <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(G-e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(G-e)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(G/e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(G/e)</annotation></semantics></math> (for various edges <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>), as the graph <math id="S1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> undergoes these changes. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, however, Alon, Bucić and Gishboliner noted that the parameters <math id="S1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(G-e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(G-e)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(G/e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(G/e)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can sometimes instantly uniquely decode the entire process, an observation which drastically simplified the analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">For the chromatic polynomial spectrum, Agol and Krushkal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> also previously employed a similar strategy to show that <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{n}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{n}|</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially with <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. An important difference in their argument is that the basic operations employed are less simple than the ones in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and so one cannot readily decode the process if their operations are used freely to construct their <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex graphs. In order to circumvent this barrier, they consider only graphs obtained through a sequence of block operations, each of which combines some of their set of basic operations. To decode the sequence of block operations from the final parameters obtained, Agol and Krushkal then appeal to a simple application of the ping–pong lemma, a basic tool from geometric group theory. See for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and the references therein or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Our argument, in some sense, unifies the two strategies from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. To prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we make use of block operations that consist of sequences of two of the basic operations used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and then establish various ping–pong lemma type criteria, in order to decode.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization of the paper.</span> The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is most pleasant when <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, so we set the stage by discussing this case first in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">After that, in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we take a first dip into the more problematic case when <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we will first address for the <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math>. Using a different kind of combinatorial argument, we will derive a lower bound by reducing the problem to the case when <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, for which the result from Section <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> applies. This argument is the content of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and works in greater generality for every non-integer real number <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq\lceil q\rceil+2</annotation></semantics></math>. It does not apply directly to lower bound <math id="S1.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> but it provides a better quantitative bound for <math id="S1.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> than Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> does in this range.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we continue the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> with a more complicated ping–pong argument showing that <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> is exponential in <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5" title="5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we then complete the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> by showing that <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math> is exponential in <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)\setminus\{1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)\setminus\{1\}</annotation></semantics></math> as well, by using a more intricate combination of block constructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Concluding remarks ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we end with some concluding remarks and a couple of natural open problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
