<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section we fix a real number <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\coloneqq-q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\coloneqq-q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>A sign-normalization and what it counts at negative integers</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the sign-normalized evaluation</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(q)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)\coloneqq(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}\,P_{G}(q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For a fixed <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, multiplying by the constant factor <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, so bounding the number of distinct values of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|=n</annotation></semantics></math>
is equivalent to bounding <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Chromatic reciprocity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, Thm. 1.2]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a loopless graph on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(m)=(-1)^{n}P_{G}(-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(m)=(-1)^{n}P_{G}(-m)</annotation></semantics></math> equals the number of pairs <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{O},f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{O},f)</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> is an acyclic orientation of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V(G)\to[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V(G)\to[m]</annotation></semantics></math> is order-preserving with respect to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\to v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\to v</annotation></semantics></math> is an oriented edge of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u)\leq f(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u)\leq f(v)</annotation></semantics></math>).
In particular, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(1)=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">acyclic orientations of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(1)=\#\{\text{acyclic orientations of }G\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For non-integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there is, in general, no literal set being counted by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>; rather <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is the polynomial extension of the counting function in Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Chromatic reciprocity [, Thm. 1.2]). ‣ 2.1. A sign-normalization and what it counts at negative integers ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
Our proof below uses only the structural consequences that persist for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>: positivity and an additive deletion–contraction recurrence.
(For a geometric perspective on these reciprocity phenomena, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.)</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A standard consequence of Whitney’s broken-circuit theorem (see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>) is that the coefficients of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> alternate in sign:
for a loopless graph <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices there exist integers <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\dots,a_{n}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\dots,a_{n}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k}a_{k}x^{n-k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^{k}a_{k}x^{n-k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Positivity for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is loopless and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Substituting <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=-\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=-\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.2. Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n}P_{G}(-\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}\lambda^{n-k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n}P_{G}(-\lambda)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}\lambda^{n-k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is strictly positive since <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Additive deletion–contraction for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-loop edge of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G-e}(\lambda)+Z_{G/e}(\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G-e}(\lambda)+Z_{G/e}(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">From deletion–contraction <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the definition (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1. A sign-normalization and what it counts at negative integers ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) we compute</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G-e}(-\lambda)-(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G}(-\lambda)=(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G-e}(-\lambda)-(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">The first term is <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G-e}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G-e}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which has the same number of vertices, while</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-(-1)^{|V(G/e)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-Z_{G/e}(\lambda)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{|V(G)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-(-1)^{|V(G/e)|}P_{G/e}(-\lambda)=-Z_{G/e}(\lambda),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">giving (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 2.4 (Additive deletion–contraction for 𝑍). ‣ 2.2. Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma</h3>

<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Feasible vectors)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge of a planar graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the feasible vector witnessed by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\lambda}(G,e)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}Z_{G/e}(\lambda)\\
Z_{G-e}(\lambda)\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\lambda}(G,e)\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}Z_{G/e}(\lambda)\\
Z_{G-e}(\lambda)\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G,e)=v_{\lambda}(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G,e)=v_{\lambda}(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from context. We call <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> an <em id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–feasible</em> vector if there exists an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex planar graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and an edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G,e)=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G,e)=v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We record a simple yet crucial lemma connecting the number of such vectors with size of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Square-root lemma)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there are at least <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> distinct <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–feasible vectors, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\sqrt{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;\sqrt{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6 (Square-root lemma). ‣ 2.3. Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> is completely analogous to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Lemma 2.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> distinct <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–feasible vectors with witnesses <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{i},e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{i},e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the multiset of second coordinates <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">If at least <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct, then, since edge-deletion preserves planarity, the values <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G_{i}-e_{i}}(\lambda)=u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G_{i}-e_{i}}(\lambda)=u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> give at least <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct evaluations coming from <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex planar graphs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Otherwise, some value <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> occurs for at least <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math> indices. For those indices the pairs <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{i},u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{i},u)</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct, hence the sums <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}+u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}+u</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Additive deletion–contraction for 𝑍). ‣ 2.2. Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}+u=Z_{G_{i}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}+u=Z_{G_{i}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, so again we obtain at least <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{N}</annotation></semantics></math> distinct values.
Finally <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)=(-1)^{n}Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)=(-1)^{n}Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|=n</annotation></semantics></math>, so the same lower bound holds for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Two basic operations and their matrices</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two operations on witnessed edges. Let <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=ab</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Basic operations)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(S)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<em id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Subdivision</em>) Subdivide <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> by a new vertex <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, replacing <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> with the path <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\!-\!w\!-\!b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\!-\!w\!-\!b</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the new graph and set <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\prime},aw)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\prime},aw)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(B)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(<em id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Add a vertex adjacent to both endpoints</em>) Add a new vertex <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to both <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> be the new graph and set <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\ast},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(G,e)\coloneqq(G^{\ast},e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We repeatedly use the standard leaf identity: if <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by attaching a new leaf to an existing vertex, then</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H}(x)=(x-1)P_{G}(x)\qquad\text{and hence}\qquad Z_{H}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G}(\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>and hence</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H}(x)=(x-1)P_{G}(x)\qquad\text{and hence}\qquad Z_{H}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G}(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Matrix action)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge of a planar graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G,e)=\binom{t}{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G,e)=\binom{t}{u}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v\circ S)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
0&amp;\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u},\qquad(v\circ B)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda+1&amp;0\\
1&amp;\lambda+2\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>t</mi><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v\circ S)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
0&amp;\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u},\qquad(v\circ B)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda+1&amp;0\\
1&amp;\lambda+2\end{pmatrix}\binom{t}{u}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=ab</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Operation <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
Let <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\prime},f)=S(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime},f)=S(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=aw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=aw</annotation></semantics></math>.
Contracting <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> merges <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and collapses the path <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\!-\!w\!-\!b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\!-\!w\!-\!b</annotation></semantics></math> back to the single edge <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\prime}/f)\cong G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime}/f)\cong G</annotation></semantics></math> and hence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G^{\prime}/f}(\lambda)=Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+u" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G^{\prime}/f}(\lambda)=Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+u</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4 (Additive deletion–contraction for 𝑍). ‣ 2.2. Positivity and an additive deletion–contraction identity ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.
Deleting <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> a leaf attached to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in the graph <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-e</annotation></semantics></math>, so by (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G^{\prime}-f}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)u." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G^{\prime}-f}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)u.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This gives the stated matrix for <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Operation <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</em>
Let <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\ast},e)=B(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\ast},e)=B(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> with new vertex <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> adjacent to both <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
Contracting <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> merges <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>; the two edges <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aw</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bw</annotation></semantics></math> become parallel edges from the merged vertex to <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, which do not affect proper colorings, so <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\ast}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\ast}/e</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/e</annotation></semantics></math> by attaching a leaf. Hence by (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G^{\ast}/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G^{\ast}/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)Z_{G/e}(\lambda)=(\lambda+1)t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For deletion, set <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\coloneqq G^{\ast}-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\coloneqq G^{\ast}-e</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim the polynomial identity</span></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H}(x)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H}(x)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, apply deletion–contraction to the edge <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aw</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Deleting <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aw</annotation></semantics></math> leaves a leaf at <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> attached to <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-e</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H-aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H-aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Contracting <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aw</annotation></semantics></math> identifies <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and turns <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bw</annotation></semantics></math> into an edge <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math>, recovering <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (up to harmless parallel edges),
so <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H/aw}(x)=P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H/aw}(x)=P_{G}(x)=P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> equals</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{H-aw}(x)-P_{H/aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x)-\bigl(P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)\bigr)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{H-aw}(x)-P_{H/aw}(x)=(x-1)P_{G-e}(x)-\bigl(P_{G-e}(x)-P_{G/e}(x)\bigr)=P_{G/e}(x)+(x-2)P_{G-e}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">proving (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
Evaluating (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) at <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=-\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=-\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and multiplying by <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{|V(H)|}=(-1)^{|V(G)|+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{|V(H)|}=(-1)^{|V(G)|+1}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{H}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+(\lambda+2)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+(\lambda+2)u." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{H}(\lambda)=Z_{G/e}(\lambda)+(\lambda+2)Z_{G-e}(\lambda)=t+(\lambda+2)u.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">This gives the stated matrix for <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In view of this lemma, we will sometimes write, say, <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> for the matrix corresponding to the operation <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\binom{x}{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\binom{x}{y}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(v)\coloneqq x/y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(v)\coloneqq x/y</annotation></semantics></math>. We also define the feasible vector witnessed after a sequence of operations as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=(a_{1},\dots,a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> be a word in the semigroup <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B,S\}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B,S\}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a planar graph, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}(a,G,e)\coloneqq(v\circ a_{t}\circ a_{t-1}\circ\dots\circ a_{1})(G,e)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}(a,G,e)\coloneqq(v\circ a_{t}\circ a_{t-1}\circ\dots\circ a_{1})(G,e),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the feasible vector witnessed by the pair generated through the sequence of operations encoded by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> starting with the pair <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove the following lemma, which guarantees that different words of the same length generate different feasible vectors if starting from an appropriate pair <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}\coloneqq v(K_{2},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}\coloneqq v(K_{2},e)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique edge of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}=\binom{\lambda}{\lambda^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>λ</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}=\binom{\lambda}{\lambda^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(v_{0})=1/\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(v_{0})=1/\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, on ratios the operations act by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(r)=\frac{r+1}{\lambda+1},\qquad f_{B}(r)=\frac{(\lambda+1)r}{r+\lambda+2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(r)=\frac{r+1}{\lambda+1},\qquad f_{B}(r)=\frac{(\lambda+1)r}{r+\lambda+2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\coloneqq(0,1/\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\coloneqq(0,1/\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(I)\subset\bigl(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(I)\subset\bigl(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B}(I)\subset\bigl(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B}(I)\subset\bigl(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>,
so the images are disjoint and contained in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\neq w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\neq w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are words in the semigroup <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{S,B\}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{S,B\}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same number of letters, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))\neq r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))\neq r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The base computation is immediate: <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}/e</annotation></semantics></math> is a single vertex graph, so <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{K_{2}/e}(\lambda)=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{K_{2}/e}(\lambda)=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}-e</annotation></semantics></math> has two isolated vertices, so <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{K_{2}-e}(\lambda)=\lambda^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{K_{2}-e}(\lambda)=\lambda^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8 (Matrix action). ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, if <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\binom{x}{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\binom{x}{y}</annotation></semantics></math> then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sv=\binom{x+y}{(\lambda+1)y},\qquad Bv=\binom{(\lambda+1)x}{x+(\lambda+2)y}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sv=\binom{x+y}{(\lambda+1)y},\qquad Bv=\binom{(\lambda+1)x}{x+(\lambda+2)y},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">so the ratio maps are <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S},f_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S},f_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now fix <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;r\leq 1/\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r\leq 1/\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(r)&gt;f(0)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}\quad\text{and}\quad f_{S}(r)\leq f_{S}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(r)&gt;f(0)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}\quad\text{and}\quad f_{S}(r)\leq f_{S}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{S}(I)\subset(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{S}(I)\subset(1/(\lambda+1),\,1/\lambda]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, since <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B}(r)&gt;f_{B}(0)=0\quad\text{and}\quad f_{B}(r)\leq f_{B}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B}(r)&gt;f_{B}(0)=0\quad\text{and}\quad f_{B}(r)\leq f_{B}(1/\lambda)=\frac{1}{\lambda+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{B}(I)\subset(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{B}(I)\subset(0,\,1/(\lambda+1)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, the images are disjoint.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, suppose <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))=r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e))=r(\mathbf{v}(w^{\prime},K_{2},e))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Observe that they must both lie in <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the image
disjointness just proved and the fact that they have the same number of letters, they are either both empty or the last letter of <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> must equal the last letter of <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the former case, <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, while in the latter case, since both matrices in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8 (Matrix action). ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> are invertible, we can cancel the last letter in each word and iterate to conclude that <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the operations defined in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7 (Basic operations). ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> preserve graph planarity and connectivity, and increase the number of vertices of the graph by one. In fact, if we apply a sequence of these operations starting with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{3},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{3},e)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{4},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{4},e)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> being an arbitrary edge of the respective graph, then it is not hard to inductively show that the resulting graph is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>–connected. Indeed, this follows from Whitney’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, since the graph will always have an ear decomposition: after applying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, replace <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-w-b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-w-b</annotation></semantics></math> within the ears containing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>; after applying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, we simply gain a new ear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, replacing the starting graph in Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{3},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{3},e)</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, and the subsequent Lemma  <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 4.5. ‣ 4.2. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, all yield <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>–connected, planar witnesses, so after applying Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6 (Square-root lemma). ‣ 2.3. Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2 (Square-root lemma). ‣ 4.1. Attainable vectors ‣ 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> respectively, we get the same bound asymptotically on the evaluation spectrum even if we require <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mG_{n}^{\pl}</annotation></semantics></math> to be connected.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove the first bound of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a real number <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-2)/2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;2^{(n-2)/2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Starting from <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K_{2},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K_{2},e)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, each application of <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> adds exactly one vertex and preserves planarity.
Thus every word <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\{S,B\}^{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\{S,B\}^{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex witness and an <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–feasible vector
<math id="S2.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}(w,K_{2},e)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, these <math id="S2.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math> vectors are all distinct.
Applying Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6 (Square-root lemma). ‣ 2.3. Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> finishes the proof:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\geq\sqrt{2^{n-2}}=2^{(n-2)/2}.\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)|\geq\sqrt{2^{n-2}}=2^{(n-2)/2}.\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is not a coincidence that Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 2.12. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a> recovers the same quantitative bound from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Corollary 2.6]</cite>. Our two basic operations <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are two out of the three operations used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and our ping–pong Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> morally substitutes their <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Theorem 2.5]</cite> in the analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is perhaps important to add that the third operation used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> to subsequently upgrade their lower bound does not behave well for our purposes. Namely, in the notation of Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5 (Feasible vectors). ‣ 2.3. Feasible vectors and a square-root lemma ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 2.7 (Basic operations). ‣ 2.4. Two basic operations and their matrices ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, if we consider the witness <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G^{\ast},wa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\ast},wa)</annotation></semantics></math>, then one can check that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(G^{\ast},wa)=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
\lambda+1&amp;\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}v(G,e)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(G^{\ast},wa)=\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\
\lambda+1&amp;\lambda+1\end{pmatrix}v(G,e).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The matrix is singular, so it is not possible to use this operation and still get a free semigroup.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we show that once the evaluation spectrum <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially for every point in <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is automatically exponentially large for every non-integer positive point. In particular, this handles the problematic range <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The reduction uses the join operation and a simple coloring-counting identity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Join)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For graphs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> on disjoint vertex sets, the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">join</em> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\vee H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\vee H</annotation></semantics></math>
is obtained from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\sqcup H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\sqcup H</annotation></semantics></math> by adding all edges between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\vee K_{m}}(x)=(x)_{m}\,P_{G}(x-m)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\vee K_{m}}(x)=(x)_{m}\,P_{G}(x-m),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(x)_{m}\coloneqq x(x-1)\cdots(x-m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{m}\coloneqq x(x-1)\cdots(x-m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the falling factorial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix an integer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>.
In a proper <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-coloring of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\vee K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\vee K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of the clique <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> must receive <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> distinct colors,
and none of those colors may appear on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (since every vertex of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>).
There are <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> ways to color <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(x-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(x-m)</annotation></semantics></math> ways to color <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> using the remaining <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-m</annotation></semantics></math> colors.
Thus (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point ‣ 3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) holds for all integers <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence as an identity of polynomials.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix a real <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q)_{m}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q)_{m}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n+m}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q-m)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n+m}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n}(q-m)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq m</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{N}(q)|\geq|\mathsf{S}_{N-m}(q-m)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{N}(q)|\geq|\mathsf{S}_{N-m}(q-m)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Define <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(G)\coloneqq G\vee K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(G)\coloneqq G\vee K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex graphs to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n+m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n+m)</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex graphs.
By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point ‣ 3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\Phi(G)}(q)=P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q)=(q)_{m}\,P_{G}(q-m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\Phi(G)}(q)=P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q)=(q)_{m}\,P_{G}(q-m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q)_{m}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q)_{m}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\mapsto(q)_{m}y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto(q)_{m}y</annotation></semantics></math> is injective on real values, so distinct values of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q-m)</annotation></semantics></math>
yield distinct values of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G\vee K_{m}}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Exponential growth for every non-integer <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can then prove the following lower bound for the chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra at non-integer positive numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-integer real and set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\coloneqq\lceil q\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\coloneqq\lceil q\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q-m\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q-m\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q)_{m}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q)_{m}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m+2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is not an integer, none of the factors <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q,q-1,\dots,q-m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q,q-1,\dots,q-m+1</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, hence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q)_{m}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q)_{m}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1&lt;q&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1&lt;q&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q-m\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q-m\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Join with a clique shifts the evaluation point ‣ 3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> with this <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and use Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 2.12. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a> at the negative point <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q-m</annotation></semantics></math>:
for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq m+2</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and hence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}.\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ |\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n-m}(q-m)|\ \geq\ 2^{(n-m-2)/2}.\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The trick to adjoin a clique discussed in this section works well to show Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2. Exponential growth for every non-integer 𝑞&gt;0 ‣ 3. Non-integer positive evaluations via clique joins ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, but it does not allow us to control certain properties of the resulting graph. In fact, if one applies the operation <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> twice or more within a sequence of operations, then the resulting graph contains a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2,3}</annotation></semantics></math>-subdivision, so joining by just one vertex destroys planarity.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The planar evaluation spectrum for <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove the last bound of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> by showing the following</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> be a real number and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{n/2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}^{\pl}_{n}(q)\right|\geq\sqrt{F_{n-2}}=\Theta(\varphi^{n/2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F_{n})_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F_{n})_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fibonacci sequence and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi=(1+\sqrt{5})/2</annotation></semantics></math> is the golden ratio. In particular, we get the bound for integers <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Attainable vectors</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a planar graph and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> one of its edges. Define the <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">attainable vector</em> witnessed by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{q}{(G,e)}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}P_{G/e}(q)\\[2.0pt]
P_{G-e}(q)\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{q}{(G,e)}\coloneqq\begin{pmatrix}P_{G/e}(q)\\[2.0pt]
P_{G-e}(q)\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(G,e)=w_{q}(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(G,e)=w_{q}(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from context.
We call <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> an <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–attainable</em> vector if there exists an <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>–vertex planar graph <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and a edge <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We remark that</p>
