</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is any edge of <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}=K_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}=K_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=m(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=m(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer to be chosen later.
We now prove that <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies properties (<a href="#S5.I1.i1" title="Item 1 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)–(<a href="#S5.I1.i3" title="Item 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(w(G_{0},e_{0}))=1/(q-1)\in(1,\infty)=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w(G_{0},e_{0}))=1/(q-1)\in(1,\infty)=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, if <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=r(w(G,e))\in(1,\infty)=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r(w(G,e))\in(1,\infty)=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=(w\circ S^{2m})(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=(w\circ S^{2m})(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(w_{k})=\frac{1}{q}+\frac{r-\frac{1}{q}}{(q-1)^{2m}}\geq\frac{1}{q}+\frac{1-\frac{1}{q}}{(q-1)^{2m}}\overset{m\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}\infty" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w_{k})=\frac{1}{q}+\frac{r-\frac{1}{q}}{(q-1)^{2m}}\geq\frac{1}{q}+\frac{1-\frac{1}{q}}{(q-1)^{2m}}\overset{m\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}\infty</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&gt;1/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&gt;1/q</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for sufficiently large <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(w_{k})\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w_{k})\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S5.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Item 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), if <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,0)=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,0)=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\ell}=(w\circ B^{2})(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\ell}=(w\circ B^{2})(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{(2q-3)+(q-2)^{2}}\leq r(w_{\ell})=\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{(2q-3)+(q-2)^{2}r^{-1}}&lt;\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{2q-3}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{(2q-3)+(q-2)^{2}}\leq r(w_{\ell})=\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{(2q-3)+(q-2)^{2}r^{-1}}&lt;\frac{(q-1)^{2}}{2q-3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;3/2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(w_{\ell})\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w_{\ell})\in J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Case 3:</em><span id="S5.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text"> <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X=(G_{0},e_{0},K,L,I,J)=\big(K_{4},e,S^{2},B\circ S^{2}\circ B,(-\infty,0],(0,1/2]\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=(G_{0},e_{0},K,L,I,J)=\big(K_{4},e,S^{2},B\circ S^{2}\circ B,(-\infty,0],(0,1/2]\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is any edge of <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}=K_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}=K_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We now prove that <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies properties (<a href="#S5.I1.i1" title="Item 1 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)–(<a href="#S5.I1.i3" title="Item 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(w(G_{0},e_{0}))=1/(q-2)=-2\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w(G_{0},e_{0}))=1/(q-2)=-2\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 5.2. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, if <math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S5.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{k}=(w\circ S^{2})(G,e)=4r-2\in(-\infty,0]=I." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=(w\circ S^{2})(G,e)=4r-2\in(-\infty,0]=I.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 4.3 (Matrix action). ‣ 4.1. Attainable vectors ‣ 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we obtain</p>
<table id="S5.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w\circ L)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1/8\\
-1/8&amp;5/16\end{pmatrix}w(G,e)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w\circ L)(G,e)=\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;1/8\\
-1/8&amp;5/16\end{pmatrix}w(G,e).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, if <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=r(w(G,e))\in(-\infty,1/2]=I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{\ell}=(w\circ L)(G,e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{\ell}=(w\circ L)(G,e)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S5.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;r(w_{u})=\frac{2}{5-2r}\leq\frac{1}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r(w_{u})=\frac{2}{5-2r}\leq\frac{1}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">since <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\mapsto 2/(5-2r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\mapsto 2/(5-2r)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing in <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\cup J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\cup J</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus
<math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(w_{\ell})\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(w_{\ell})\in J</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to prove Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G_{0},e_{0},K,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{0},e_{0},K,L)</annotation></semantics></math> be given by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and let <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k(q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\ell(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\ell(q)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of basic operations in blocks <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Let <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\smash{\lfloor\frac{n-|V(G_{0}|)}{(k+\ell)}\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\smash{\lfloor\frac{n-|V(G_{0}|)}{(k+\ell)}\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math> and notice that <math id="S5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=|V(G_{0})|+t(k+\ell)\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=|V(G_{0})|+t(k+\ell)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, there are at least</span></p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{2t}{t}\geq\frac{2^{2t}}{2t+1}=2^{\Omega_{q}(n)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{2t}{t}\geq\frac{2^{2t}}{2t+1}=2^{\Omega_{q}(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">distinct <math id="S5.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>–attainable vectors, since words in <math id="S5.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{K,L\}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{K,L\}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S5.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S5.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>’s produce distinct <math id="S5.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>–attainable vectors. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n_{0}}(q)|\;=\;2^{\Omega_{q}(n)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n}(q)|\;\geq\;|\mathsf{S}^{\mathrm{pl}}_{n_{0}}(q)|\;=\;2^{\Omega_{q}(n)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the left inequality comes from the fact that adding a leaf to a connected planar graph preserves planarity and alters the chromatic polynomial by a factor of <math id="S5.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the right identity is given by (<a href="#S5.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Proof of Theorem . ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) and Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 4.2 (Square-root lemma). ‣ 4.1. Attainable vectors ‣ 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(The dependence on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> or lack thereof)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> as proven only gives that for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,2)\setminus\left\{1\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,2)\setminus\left\{1\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> the existence of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(q)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(q)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq\exp\left((c(q)+o(1))n\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq\exp\left((c(q)+o(1))n\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, a more detailed analysis easily can show that we may choose <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(q)</annotation></semantics></math> to be an absolute constant, namely <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ul id="S5.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(0,1.458)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.458</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(0,1.458)</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction in Case 1 of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> works with <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction provides the bound <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/4-o(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/4-o(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(1,3/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(1,3/2]</annotation></semantics></math>, one can use the construction in Case 3 of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. This construction provides the bound <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(3/2,1.53)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1.53</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(3/2,1.53)</annotation></semantics></math>, one can slightly modify the construction in Case 3 of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, picking</p>
<table id="S5.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(G_{0},e_{0},K,L,I,J)=\big(K_{4},e,S^{4},B\circ S^{2}\circ B,(-\infty,-0.5],(-0.5,0.56]\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.56</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G_{0},e_{0},K,L,I,J)=\big(K_{4},e,S^{4},B\circ S^{2}\circ B,(-\infty,-0.5],(-0.5,0.56]\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.I5.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This choice provides the bound <math id="S5.I5.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/8-o(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/8-o(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.I5.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in[1.53,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1.53</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in[1.53,2]</annotation></semantics></math>, the construction in Case 2 of Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> works with <math id="S5.I5.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction provides the bound <math id="S5.I5.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>pl</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathsf{S}_{n}^{\pl}(q)\right|\geq 2^{n/6-o(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We leave the details to the interested reader. Notice that Theorems <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 2.12. ‣ 2.5. Ping–pong on ratios and exponential growth ‣ 2. Negative evaluations and chromatic reciprocity ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The planar evaluation spectrum for 𝑞&gt;2 ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, under the observations of Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Remark 5.4 (The dependence on 𝑞 or lack thereof). ‣ 5. A planar ping–pong for 𝑞∈(0,2)∖{1} ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, imply Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Chromatic polynomial evaluation spectra" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Concluding remarks</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Superexponential spectra.</span> For every fixed real <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\notin\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\notin\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> we proved that the evaluation spectrum</p>
<table id="S6.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;=\;\{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)\;=\;\{P_{G}(q):|V(G)|=n\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">grows exponentially in <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, with explicit lower bounds of different qualities in various regimes. For positive integers <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\not\in\left\{0,1,2\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also noted that exponential growth for the evaluation spectrum of <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> is best possible (up to the correct base of the exponent), due to the fact that <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)|\leq q^{n}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, at other values of <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, the size of <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{n}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> could be significantly larger, in principle. For example, when <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{G}(-1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{G}(-1)|</annotation></semantics></math> counts the number of acyclic orientations of <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, so the natural upper bound for <math id="S6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)|</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{K_{n}}(-1)|=n!</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id="S6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{n}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{n}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this direction, we would like to make the appealing following conjecture for the spectrum of acyclic orientations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)|\ \text{is superexponential in\ }n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">is superexponential in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathsf{S}_{n}(-1)|\ \text{is superexponential in\ }n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A closely related “spectrum” question concerns the unrestrained spanning–tree spectrum <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. We discussed the recent works of Chan–Kontorovich–Pak and of Alon–Bucić–Gishboliner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> which show that <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>pl</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}^{\pl}(n)|</annotation></semantics></math> is exponential in <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, if one restricts the count to planar graphs. In the unrestricted setting, however, the extremal values are also superexponential (recall Cayley’s theorem that <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(K_{n})=n^{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(K_{n})=n^{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>), so it is a natural to conjecture that <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}(n)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}(n)|</annotation></semantics></math> is also superexponential. In this direction, we would like to reiterate the very appealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Conjecture 5.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{T}(n)|=e^{\Omega(n\log n)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{T}(n)|=e^{\Omega(n\log n)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How far does the ping–pong method go?</span>
Our arguments use remarkably little about chromatic polynomials beyond the existence of a two–term deletion–contraction relation
and the ability to realize local operations as linear maps on a small “boundary” space.
This suggests that similar results should be possible for a broader class of Tutte–type invariants.
For example, the Tutte polynomial <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> (and equivalently the Potts/random–cluster partition function) is universal among
Tutte–Grothendieck invariants and is governed by deletion–contraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
It would be interesting to study evaluation spectra such as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\,T_{G}(x_{0},y_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\{\,Z_{G}(q_{0},v_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\,T_{G}(x_{0},y_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\}\qquad\text{or}\qquad\{\,Z_{G}(q_{0},v_{0}):|V(G)|=n\,\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">at fixed parameters, and to understand when the same feasible–vector/ping–pong mechanism yields exponential (or even superexponential) growth. Here <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(q,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(q,v)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the random–cluster (Potts) partition function, a standard specialization of the Tutte polynomial. In particular, it would be interesting to determine the specializations of the Tutte polynomial (or more general Tutte–Grothendieck invariants) that guarantee
exponential growth of the corresponding evaluation spectrum, as well as the exceptional locus where the spectrum is subexponential.</p>
</div>
